                    ('michael-the-gadite-forefather', $$This Michael stands several generations back in Abihail's pedigree, where the record the reading chose is anchored to the contemporary named in verse 13.$$,
                     $$This Michael stands several generations back in Abihail's pedigree, and is not the Michael named among the brothers in verse 13.$$),
                    ('hepher-the-mecherathite', $$In the parallel list of 2 Samuel 23:34 the Eliam of the encyclopedia's record answers to Ahijah the Pelonite, the next name here; Hepher answers to Eliphelet's Maachathite epithet. The two lists name him and no record holds him.$$,
                     $$In the parallel list of 2 Samuel 23:34 Eliam answers to Ahijah the Pelonite, the next name here; Hepher answers to Eliphelet's Maachathite epithet.$$),
                    ('zechariah-the-trumpeting-priest', $$1 Chronicles 15:24 lists the men who blew the trumpets before the ark as priests, a different group from the second-degree Levite singers of verses 18 to 20. The encyclopedia's record carries both verses, so it is holding two men under one name.$$,
                     $$1 Chronicles 15:24 lists the men who blew the trumpets before the ark as priests, a different group from the second-degree Levite singers of verses 18 to 20.$$),
                    ('jehoiada-son-of-benaiah', $$The verse names Jehoiada as the son of Benaiah, where the record the reading chose is Benaiah's father. Whether the chronicler has transposed the names or is naming a grandson is argued both ways, and both readings agree that the record as it stands does not hold this man.$$,
                     $$The verse names Jehoiada as the son of Benaiah, while the Jehoiada best known is Benaiah's father. Whether the chronicler has transposed the names or is naming a grandson is argued both ways, and on either reading this Jehoiada is not Benaiah's father.$$),
                    ('uriel-of-gibeah', $$Kings traces Abijah's mother to Abishalom and Chronicles to Uriel of Gibeah. Whether that is one line or two is old and unsettled, and the encyclopedia answers it silently by merging the two names into one record.$$,
                     $$Kings traces Abijah's mother to Abishalom and Chronicles to Uriel of Gibeah. Whether that is one line or two is old and unsettled.$$),
                    ('zechariah-who-taught-uzziah', $$The counsellor who instructed Uzziah in the eighth century stands two centuries before the post-exilic prophet of the book of Zechariah, and the encyclopedia's record carries both.$$,
