                    ('jehiel-ruler-of-the-house-of-god', $$Josiah's passover is some ninety years after Hezekiah's cleansing of the temple, and these rulers give to the priests rather than standing among the Hemanite Levites the record is anchored to.$$,
                     $$Josiah's passover is some ninety years after Hezekiah's cleansing of the temple, and these rulers give to the priests rather than standing among the sons of Heman who served at Hezekiah's cleansing (2CH 29:14).$$),
                    ('azariah-the-covenant-priest', $$The encyclopedia offered two men of this name and both are centuries out: azariah-7 is the son of Hilkiah in the pre-exilic high-priestly line, and azariah-15 is Hezekiah's chief priest. Neither can be sealing a covenant in Jerusalem in the fifth century. That azariah-7 carries this verse in its notes is the source contradicting its own distinguisher rather than evidence about the man.$$,
                     $$He is neither of two earlier men of this name: Azariah the son of Hilkiah in the high-priestly line before the exile (1CH 6:13), and Hezekiah's chief priest Azariah (2CH 31:10). Neither can be sealing a covenant in Jerusalem in the fifth century.$$),
                    ('obadiah-the-teaching-prince', $$The encyclopedia offered Ahab's steward in the northern kingdom and a post-exilic Levite; neither is a prince of Judah under Jehoshaphat, and Strong keeps the prince as a separate man. That the steward's record names this verse in its own distinguisher is the source merging two men, not evidence that they are one.$$,
                     $$A prince of Judah under Jehoshaphat (2CH 17:7), neither Ahab's steward in the northern kingdom nor a Levite after the exile; Strong keeps the prince as a man of his own.$$),
                    ('zimri-in-jezebels-cry', $$Jezebel's words can be read two ways and the corpus records the rhetoric rather than resolving it: she may be naming the historical usurper who killed his master and reigned seven days, or she may be calling Jehu himself a second Zimri. The encyclopedia's own data carries the vocative reading, by giving Jehu a name label of Zimri.$$,
                     $$Jezebel's words can be read two ways: she may be naming the historical usurper who killed his master and reigned seven days, or she may be calling Jehu himself a second Zimri.$$),
                    ('jerioth', $$1 Chronicles 2:18 reads, word for word, "And Caleb son of Hezron begat Azubah a woman and Jerioth, and these are her sons: Jesher and Shobab and Ardon." Two things in it are what make the name hard, and both can be read off the Hebrew this corpus holds.$$ || chr(10) || $$$$ || chr(10) || $$The first is that the sons are hers, not theirs: בָנֶיהָ carries the feminine singular suffix, and the King James prints "her sons are these" for that reason. One woman is meant, and the verse has just named two.$$ || chr(10) || $$$$ || chr(10) || $$The second is the particle. Azubah is introduced by אֵת as the object of the verb (H853), and Jerioth by אֵת the preposition, "with" (H854) — two lexemes written with the same consonants, which BHSA and Strong's Dictionary distinguish and the pointing does not. So the clause can be read as begetting Azubah and Jerioth alike, or as begetting Azubah's sons with Jerioth, and nothing in the sentence decides between them.$$ || chr(10) || $$$$ || chr(10) || $$What the encyclopedia holds is therefore this: Azubah is Caleb's wife and Jesher, Shobab and Ardon are her sons; Jerioth is a person named in the same breath whom the verse does not identify. Who else she might be, and why, is listed beside this record rather than chosen between. The record keeps her as a woman, as every reading that takes the word to name or describe somebody does but one: read as the name of Azubah's father, it is a man's. The name itself is the plural of יְרִיעָה, a curtain of the kind the tabernacle was hung with (H3407), which is what the reading that takes it for a description rather than a name rests on.$$,
                     $$1 Chronicles 2:18 reads, word for word, "And Caleb son of Hezron begat Azubah a woman and Jerioth, and these are her sons: Jesher and Shobab and Ardon." Two things in it are what make the name hard, and both can be read off the Hebrew.$$ || chr(10) || $$$$ || chr(10) || $$The first is that the sons are hers, not theirs: בָנֶיהָ carries the feminine singular suffix, and the King James prints "her sons are these" for that reason. One woman is meant, and the verse has just named two.$$ || chr(10) || $$$$ || chr(10) || $$The second is the particle. Azubah is introduced by אֵת as the object of the verb (H853), and Jerioth by אֵת the preposition, "with" (H854) — two lexemes written with the same consonants, which BHSA and Strong's Dictionary distinguish and the pointing does not. So the clause can be read as begetting Azubah and Jerioth alike, or as begetting Azubah's sons with Jerioth, and nothing in the sentence decides between them.$$ || chr(10) || $$$$ || chr(10) || $$What can be said is therefore this: Azubah is Caleb's wife and Jesher, Shobab and Ardon are her sons; Jerioth is a person named in the same breath whom the verse does not identify. Who else she might be, and why, is listed beside her rather than chosen between. She is held to be a woman, as every reading that takes the word to name or describe somebody does but one: read as the name of Azubah's father, it is a man's. The name itself is the plural of יְרִיעָה, a curtain of the kind the tabernacle was hung with (H3407), which is what the reading that takes it for a description rather than a name rests on.$$),
                    ('israelites', $$The record for the man is jacob, which is where the patriarch's own life belongs. This is the people, and a word naming the nation reaches here rather than reaching a page about a birth in Genesis.$$,
                     $$The people descended from Jacob, whom God named Israel (GEN 32:28), and called by his name.$$),
                    ('levites', $$Strong's King James list spells the gentilic Leviite, which is a slip of the printed dictionary rather than a name anybody uses. The record is named Levites.$$,
                     $$Strong's King James list spells the gentilic Leviite, which is a slip of the printed dictionary rather than a name anybody uses.$$),
                    ('cherubim', $$When he had driven out the man, the LORD God placed at the east of the garden of Eden cherubims, and a flaming sword which turned every way, to keep the way of the tree of life (GEN 3:24). Genesis does not describe them. Ezekiel saw them by the river Chebar and says, I knew that they were the cherubims (EZK 10:15, 20): every one had four faces and four wings, and the likeness of a man's hands under their wings (EZK 10:21; 1:5-11), with a wheel beside each (EZK 10:9-13); in 10:14 the first face is the face of a cherub where 1:10 has the face of an ox. Two cherubim of beaten gold were made at the two ends of the mercy seat, stretching forth their wings on high and their faces one to another (EXO 25:18-20; 37:7-9), and from between them God spoke with Moses (EXO 25:22; NUM 7:89); the LORD of hosts is called he which dwelleth between the cherubims where the ark is named (1SA 4:4; 2SA 6:2). Cherubim were also woven into the curtains and the veil of the tabernacle (EXO 26:1, 31), carved and set up in Solomon's temple (1KI 6:23-35) and carved on the walls of the temple of Ezekiel's vision (EZK 41:18-20); those are the work's figures of them and are not linked to this record. The prince of Tyre is addressed as the anointed cherub that covereth (EZK 28:14, 16), a figure of speech.$$,
                     $$When he had driven out the man, the LORD God placed at the east of the garden of Eden cherubims, and a flaming sword which turned every way, to keep the way of the tree of life (GEN 3:24). Genesis does not describe them. Ezekiel saw them by the river Chebar and says, I knew that they were the cherubims (EZK 10:15, 20): every one had four faces and four wings, and the likeness of a man's hands under their wings (EZK 10:21; 1:5-11), with a wheel beside each (EZK 10:9-13); in 10:14 the first face is the face of a cherub where 1:10 has the face of an ox. Two cherubim of beaten gold were made at the two ends of the mercy seat, stretching forth their wings on high and their faces one to another (EXO 25:18-20; 37:7-9), and from between them God spoke with Moses (EXO 25:22; NUM 7:89); the LORD of hosts is called he which dwelleth between the cherubims where the ark is named (1SA 4:4; 2SA 6:2). Cherubim were also woven into the curtains and the veil of the tabernacle (EXO 26:1, 31), carved and set up in Solomon's temple (1KI 6:23-35) and carved on the walls of the temple of Ezekiel's vision (EZK 41:18-20); those are the work's figures of them. The prince of Tyre is addressed as the anointed cherub that covereth (EZK 28:14, 16), a figure of speech.$$),
                    ('new-jerusalem', $$Jesus promises him that overcometh to write upon him the name of the city of my God, which is new Jerusalem, which cometh down out of heaven from my God (REV 3:12). John saw the holy city, new Jerusalem, coming down from God out of heaven, prepared as a bride adorned for her husband (REV 21:2), and was carried away in the spirit to a great and high mountain and shown that great city, the holy Jerusalem, descending out of heaven from God, having the glory of God (REV 21:10-11). It had a wall great and high with twelve gates, at the gates twelve angels, and on them the names of the twelve tribes of the children of Israel; the wall had twelve foundations, and in them the names of the twelve apostles of the Lamb (REV 21:12-14). The angel measured the city with a golden reed: it lieth foursquare, twelve thousand furlongs, the length and the breadth and the height of it equal, and the wall an hundred and forty and four cubits (REV 21:15-17). The wall was of jasper and the city pure gold, like unto clear glass; the foundations were garnished with twelve precious stones, and the twelve gates were twelve pearls (REV 21:18-21). John saw no temple therein, for the Lord God Almighty and the Lamb are the temple of it, and the city had no need of the sun, neither of the moon (REV 21:22-23); the river of water of life proceeded out of the throne, and on either side of the river was the tree of life (REV 22:1-2). It is seen in a vision and has no place on a map. Paul speaks of Jerusalem which is above (GAL 4:26) and Hebrews of the heavenly Jerusalem (HEB 12:22); those verses do not call it new and are not linked to this record.$$,
                     $$Jesus promises him that overcometh to write upon him the name of the city of my God, which is new Jerusalem, which cometh down out of heaven from my God (REV 3:12). John saw the holy city, new Jerusalem, coming down from God out of heaven, prepared as a bride adorned for her husband (REV 21:2), and was carried away in the spirit to a great and high mountain and shown that great city, the holy Jerusalem, descending out of heaven from God, having the glory of God (REV 21:10-11). It had a wall great and high with twelve gates, at the gates twelve angels, and on them the names of the twelve tribes of the children of Israel; the wall had twelve foundations, and in them the names of the twelve apostles of the Lamb (REV 21:12-14). The angel measured the city with a golden reed: it lieth foursquare, twelve thousand furlongs, the length and the breadth and the height of it equal, and the wall an hundred and forty and four cubits (REV 21:15-17). The wall was of jasper and the city pure gold, like unto clear glass; the foundations were garnished with twelve precious stones, and the twelve gates were twelve pearls (REV 21:18-21). John saw no temple therein, for the Lord God Almighty and the Lamb are the temple of it, and the city had no need of the sun, neither of the moon (REV 21:22-23); the river of water of life proceeded out of the throne, and on either side of the river was the tree of life (REV 22:1-2). It is seen in a vision and has no place on a map. Paul speaks of Jerusalem which is above (GAL 4:26) and Hebrews of the heavenly Jerusalem (HEB 12:22); those verses do not call it new.$$),
                    ('altar-of-incense', $$It was a cubit long and a cubit broad, two cubits high, with horns, overlaid with pure gold, with a crown of gold, rings and staves (EXO 30:1-5; 37:25-28), and so it is also called the golden altar (EXO 39:38; 40:26; NUM 4:11). It stood before the veil that is by the ark of the testimony (EXO 30:6; 40:5, 40:26). Aaron burned sweet incense on it every morning and evening; no strange incense, burnt offering or drink offering was to be put on it, and once a year atonement was made on its horns with blood (EXO 30:7-10), as the blood of the sin offering for the priest or the congregation was put on its horns (LEV 4:7, 4:18). Solomon made an altar of gold for the temple (1KI 7:48), where Uzziah went in to burn incense on the altar of incense and was struck with leprosy (2CH 26:16-19), and Zacharias saw an angel standing on the right side of the altar of incense (LUK 1:11). Hebrews places a golden thymiaterion with the most holy place (HEB 9:4); KJV, Ohienko, Luther and Reina-Valera all render it as a censer, and whether it means this altar is left for review.$$,
                     $$It was a cubit long and a cubit broad, two cubits high, with horns, overlaid with pure gold, with a crown of gold, rings and staves (EXO 30:1-5; 37:25-28), and so it is also called the golden altar (EXO 39:38; 40:26; NUM 4:11). It stood before the veil that is by the ark of the testimony (EXO 30:6; 40:5, 40:26). Aaron burned sweet incense on it every morning and evening; no strange incense, burnt offering or drink offering was to be put on it, and once a year atonement was made on its horns with blood (EXO 30:7-10), as the blood of the sin offering for the priest or the congregation was put on its horns (LEV 4:7, 4:18). Solomon made an altar of gold for the temple (1KI 7:48), where Uzziah went in to burn incense on the altar of incense and was struck with leprosy (2CH 26:16-19), and Zacharias saw an angel standing on the right side of the altar of incense (LUK 1:11). Hebrews places a golden thymiaterion with the most holy place (HEB 9:4); KJV, Ohienko, Luther and Reina-Valera all render it as a censer, and whether it means this altar is an open question.$$),
                    ('bronze-altar', $$It was five cubits long and five broad, foursquare, and three cubits high, of acacia wood overlaid with bronze, with horns at its four corners, a grate of bronze network, rings and staves, and hollow with boards (EXO 27:1-8; 38:1-7). It was set before the door of the tabernacle of the tent of the congregation, with the laver between it and the tent (EXO 40:6-7, 40:29-30); on it the burnt offerings were burned, and the blood of the sin offerings was put on its horns and poured out at its bottom (LEV 4:25, 4:30). The censers of Korah's company were beaten into broad plates to cover it, as a memorial to Israel (NUM 16:38-40). In David's and Solomon's day it stood with the tabernacle at the high place at Gibeon, where Solomon offered a thousand burnt offerings on it (1CH 21:29; 2CH 1:5-6). Solomon then made a bronze altar twenty cubits square for the temple (2CH 4:1), which Ahaz later moved aside for an altar copied from Damascus (2KI 16:10-15); whether those later altars belong to this record is left for review.$$,
                     $$It was five cubits long and five broad, foursquare, and three cubits high, of acacia wood overlaid with bronze, with horns at its four corners, a grate of bronze network, rings and staves, and hollow with boards (EXO 27:1-8; 38:1-7). It was set before the door of the tabernacle of the tent of the congregation, with the laver between it and the tent (EXO 40:6-7, 40:29-30); on it the burnt offerings were burned, and the blood of the sin offerings was put on its horns and poured out at its bottom (LEV 4:25, 4:30). The censers of Korah's company were beaten into broad plates to cover it, as a memorial to Israel (NUM 16:38-40). In David's and Solomon's day it stood with the tabernacle at the high place at Gibeon, where Solomon offered a thousand burnt offerings on it (1CH 21:29; 2CH 1:5-6). Solomon then made a bronze altar twenty cubits square for the temple (2CH 4:1), which Ahaz later moved aside for an altar copied from Damascus (2KI 16:10-15).$$),
                    ('cross-of-christ', $$John says Jesus went out bearing his cross to the place called Golgotha, where they crucified him (JHN 19:17-18); the other gospels say the soldiers compelled Simon of Cyrene to carry it (MAT 27:32; MRK 15:21), Luke that they laid it on him to bear after Jesus (LUK 23:26). Pilate wrote a title and put it on the cross (JHN 19:19). Passers-by and the chief priests mocked him, 'come down from the cross' (MAT 27:40-42; MRK 15:30-32). His mother, her sister, Mary the wife of Cleophas and Mary Magdalene stood by it (JHN 19:25), and because of the sabbath the Jews asked that the bodies should not remain on the cross (JHN 19:31). Paul's and Hebrews' use of 'the cross' for the event and its meaning is left for review.$$,
                     $$John says Jesus went out bearing his cross to the place called Golgotha, where they crucified him (JHN 19:17-18); the other gospels say the soldiers compelled Simon of Cyrene to carry it (MAT 27:32; MRK 15:21), Luke that they laid it on him to bear after Jesus (LUK 23:26). Pilate wrote a title and put it on the cross (JHN 19:19). Passers-by and the chief priests mocked him, 'come down from the cross' (MAT 27:40-42; MRK 15:30-32). His mother, her sister, Mary the wife of Cleophas and Mary Magdalene stood by it (JHN 19:25), and because of the sabbath the Jews asked that the bodies should not remain on the cross (JHN 19:31).$$),
                    ('moses-staff', $$At the bush the LORD asked Moses what was in his hand, and it was a rod; cast on the ground it became a serpent, and taken by the tail it became a rod again (EXO 4:2-4). He was told to take it in his hand to do the signs (EXO 4:17), and he returned to Egypt with 'the rod of God' in his hand (EXO 4:20). Moses stretched it out for the hail and the locusts (EXO 9:23; 10:13), was told to lift it up over the sea to divide it (EXO 14:16), and struck the rock at Horeb with the rod 'wherewith thou smotest the river' (EXO 17:5-6). During the battle with Amalek he stood on the hill with the rod of God in his hand (EXO 17:9). In EXO 7-8 several signs are done with Aaron's rod, and the text alternates between the two brothers in a way this record does not settle.$$,
                     $$At the bush the LORD asked Moses what was in his hand, and it was a rod; cast on the ground it became a serpent, and taken by the tail it became a rod again (EXO 4:2-4). He was told to take it in his hand to do the signs (EXO 4:17), and he returned to Egypt with 'the rod of God' in his hand (EXO 4:20). Moses stretched it out for the hail and the locusts (EXO 9:23; 10:13), was told to lift it up over the sea to divide it (EXO 14:16), and struck the rock at Horeb with the rod 'wherewith thou smotest the river' (EXO 17:5-6). During the battle with Amalek he stood on the hill with the rod of God in his hand (EXO 17:9). In EXO 7-8 several signs are done with Aaron's rod, and the text alternates between the two brothers without always saying whose rod it is.$$),
                    ('silver-trumpets', $$The LORD told Moses to make two trumpets of silver, each of a whole piece, to call the assembly and to set the camps on the move (NUM 10:2). Both blown together gathered the whole assembly at the door of the tabernacle, one alone the princes, and an alarm set the camps forward (NUM 10:3-7). The sons of Aaron, the priests, were to blow them, as an ordinance for ever (NUM 10:8). They were blown for an alarm in war, and over the burnt offerings and peace offerings on days of gladness, solemn days and new moons, for a memorial before God (NUM 10:9-10). Later passages have priests blowing trumpets without saying these are the same two; those are left for review.$$,
                     $$The LORD told Moses to make two trumpets of silver, each of a whole piece, to call the assembly and to set the camps on the move (NUM 10:2). Both blown together gathered the whole assembly at the door of the tabernacle, one alone the princes, and an alarm set the camps forward (NUM 10:3-7). The sons of Aaron, the priests, were to blow them, as an ordinance for ever (NUM 10:8). They were blown for an alarm in war, and over the burnt offerings and peace offerings on days of gladness, solemn days and new moons, for a memorial before God (NUM 10:9-10). Later passages have priests blowing trumpets without saying these are the same two.$$),
                    ('table-of-showbread', $$It was made of acacia wood two cubits long, a cubit broad and a cubit and a half high, overlaid with pure gold, with a crown and border, rings and staves, and dishes, spoons, covers and bowls of gold (EXO 25:23-29; 37:10-16). It stood in the tent of the congregation on the north side, outside the veil, and bread was to be set on it before the LORD always (EXO 25:30; 26:35; 40:22-23). Leviticus orders twelve cakes of fine flour in two rows with pure frankincense, set out anew every sabbath and eaten by Aaron and his sons in the holy place (LEV 24:5-9); on the march it was covered with blue and the continual bread stayed on it (NUM 4:7). At Nob the priest gave David this bread, taken from before the LORD, when there was no other (1SA 21:6), which Jesus cites (MAT 12:3-4; MRK 2:25-26; LUK 6:3-4). For the temple Solomon made a table of gold, and Chronicles speaks of ten tables (1KI 7:48; 2CH 4:8, 4:19); Hebrews names the table and the bread in the holy place (HEB 9:2). This record holds the bread as well as the table.$$,
                     $$It was made of acacia wood two cubits long, a cubit broad and a cubit and a half high, overlaid with pure gold, with a crown and border, rings and staves, and dishes, spoons, covers and bowls of gold (EXO 25:23-29; 37:10-16). It stood in the tent of the congregation on the north side, outside the veil, and bread was to be set on it before the LORD always (EXO 25:30; 26:35; 40:22-23). Leviticus orders twelve cakes of fine flour in two rows with pure frankincense, set out anew every sabbath and eaten by Aaron and his sons in the holy place (LEV 24:5-9); on the march it was covered with blue and the continual bread stayed on it (NUM 4:7). At Nob the priest gave David this bread, taken from before the LORD, when there was no other (1SA 21:6), which Jesus cites (MAT 12:3-4; MRK 2:25-26; LUK 6:3-4). For the temple Solomon made a table of gold, and Chronicles speaks of ten tables (1KI 7:48; 2CH 4:8, 4:19); Hebrews names the table and the bread in the holy place (HEB 9:2).$$),
                    ('tablets-of-the-covenant', $$On Sinai the LORD gave Moses two tablets of the testimony, tablets of stone written with the finger of God on both their sides (EXO 31:18; 32:15-16). Coming down and seeing the calf, Moses cast them out of his hands and broke them beneath the mount (EXO 32:19; DEU 9:17). The LORD had him hew two more like the first and wrote on them the words of the covenant, the ten commandments (EXO 34:1-4, 34:28; DEU 10:1-4), and Moses put them in the ark he had made (DEU 10:5). When Solomon's priests brought the ark into the temple there was nothing in it but these two tablets (1KI 8:9; 2CH 5:10), and Hebrews lists them among its contents (HEB 9:4). The text calls the second pair 'like unto the first' (EXO 34:1), and this record holds both pairs.$$,
                     $$On Sinai the LORD gave Moses two tablets of the testimony, tablets of stone written with the finger of God on both their sides (EXO 31:18; 32:15-16). Coming down and seeing the calf, Moses cast them out of his hands and broke them beneath the mount (EXO 32:19; DEU 9:17). The LORD had him hew two more like the first and wrote on them the words of the covenant, the ten commandments (EXO 34:1-4, 34:28; DEU 10:1-4), and Moses put them in the ark he had made (DEU 10:5). When Solomon's priests brought the ark into the temple there was nothing in it but these two tablets (1KI 8:9; 2CH 5:10), and Hebrews lists them among its contents (HEB 9:4). The text calls the second pair 'like unto the first' (EXO 34:1).$$),
                    ('feast-of-dedication', $$John records that it was the feast of the dedication at Jerusalem, that it was winter, and that Jesus walked in the temple in Solomon's porch, where the Jews asked him to say plainly whether he was the Christ (JHN 10:22-24). This is the only mention of the feast in the Bible. Its origin, the rededication of the temple after its profanation, is told in 1 Maccabees 4:36-59, a book outside this corpus. The Hebrew word for dedication used of the altar, the wall and the house in the Old Testament (NUM 7:10; NEH 12:27; the title of PSA 30) never names this feast.$$,
                     $$John records that it was the feast of the dedication at Jerusalem, that it was winter, and that Jesus walked in the temple in Solomon's porch, where the Jews asked him to say plainly whether he was the Christ (JHN 10:22-24). This is the only mention of the feast in the Bible. Its origin, the rededication of the temple after its profanation, is told in 1 Maccabees 4:36-59. The Hebrew word for dedication used of the altar, the wall and the house in the Old Testament (NUM 7:10; NEH 12:27; the title of PSA 30) never names this feast.$$),
                    ('lords-day', $$John writes that he was in the isle called Patmos and was in the Spirit on the Lord's day when he heard behind him a great voice like a trumpet (REV 1:9-10). The adjective 'the Lord's' (kyriakos) is used in the New Testament only here and in 'the Lord's supper' (1CO 11:20). The text does not say which day of the week this was. It is a different expression from 'the day of the LORD', the prophets' day of judgment (JOL 2:31; 1TH 5:2), which is not this record.$$,
                     $$John writes that he was in the isle called Patmos and was in the Spirit on the Lord's day when he heard behind him a great voice like a trumpet (REV 1:9-10). The adjective 'the Lord's' (kyriakos) is used in the New Testament only here and in 'the Lord's supper' (1CO 11:20). The text does not say which day of the week this was. It is a different expression from 'the day of the LORD', the prophets' day of judgment (JOL 2:31; 1TH 5:2).$$),
                    ('abimelech', $$The text calls the king of Gerar Abimelech when Abraham sojourns there (GEN 20:1-18) and makes a covenant with him at Beersheba (GEN 21:22-32), and again when Isaac sojourns there after Abraham's death (GEN 26:1-31). The title of Psalm 34 gives the name to the king before whom David feigned madness, whom 1 Samuel 21:10-15 calls Achish king of Gath. Read as one man, he would reach from Abraham to David, which the text nowhere asks for. The likelier reading is that Abimelech, 'my father is king', was a title of the Philistine kings as Pharaoh was of Egypt's, and Jewish tradition reads it so (Rashi). The encyclopedia therefore holds it as a title borne by possibly more than one king, and does not decide whether the Abimelech of Abraham and the Abimelech of Isaac are one man or two. It is not the Abimelech son of Gideon (JDG 8:31-9:57), whose own name it was. In 1 Chronicles 18:16 the Masoretic text also reads Abimelech, for the priest the parallel 2 Samuel 8:17 calls Ahimelech son of Abiathar; that occurrence stays with him.$$,
                     $$The text calls the king of Gerar Abimelech when Abraham sojourns there (GEN 20:1-18) and makes a covenant with him at Beersheba (GEN 21:22-32), and again when Isaac sojourns there after Abraham's death (GEN 26:1-31). The title of Psalm 34 gives the name to the king before whom David feigned madness, whom 1 Samuel 21:10-15 calls Achish king of Gath. Read as one man, he would reach from Abraham to David, which the text nowhere asks for. The likelier reading is that Abimelech, 'my father is king', was a title of the Philistine kings as Pharaoh was of Egypt's, and Jewish tradition reads it so (Rashi). It may therefore be a title borne by more than one king, and the text does not decide whether the Abimelech of Abraham and the Abimelech of Isaac are one man or two. It is not the Abimelech son of Gideon (JDG 8:31-9:57), whose own name it was. In 1 Chronicles 18:16 the Masoretic text also reads Abimelech, for the priest the parallel 2 Samuel 8:17 calls Ahimelech son of Abiathar; that verse means him.$$),
                    ('pharaoh-title', $$Pharaoh, Hebrew par'oh, is the Egyptian per-aa, 'great house', the palace and then the king who held it; the text uses it as the Egyptian court did, as the way to speak of the reigning king. The king who took Sarai (GEN 12), Joseph's king (GEN 37-50), the king of the oppression (EXO 1-2), the king of the Exodus (EXO 3-15), Solomon's father-in-law (1KI 3:1), the father of Bithiah (1CH 4:18), the king of Isaiah's oracle (ISA 19:11) and the king of Jeremiah's day (JER 25:19) are each called Pharaoh and none of them by a name, so the encyclopedia keeps each episode's king as his own record and this title as what they have in common. Twice the text gives the title and the name together: Pharaoh-nechoh (2KI 23:29) and Pharaoh-hophra (JER 44:30). Shishak and So are called king of Egypt and never Pharaoh, and are not joined here.$$,
                     $$Pharaoh, Hebrew par'oh, is the Egyptian per-aa, 'great house', the palace and then the king who held it; the text uses it as the Egyptian court did, as the way to speak of the reigning king. The king who took Sarai (GEN 12), Joseph's king (GEN 37-50), the king of the oppression (EXO 1-2), the king of the Exodus (EXO 3-15), Solomon's father-in-law (1KI 3:1), the father of Bithiah (1CH 4:18), the king of Isaiah's oracle (ISA 19:11) and the king of Jeremiah's day (JER 25:19) are each called Pharaoh and none of them by a name; each is a man of his own, and the title is what they have in common. Twice the text gives the title and the name together: Pharaoh-nechoh (2KI 23:29) and Pharaoh-hophra (JER 44:30). Shishak and So are called king of Egypt and never Pharaoh.$$),
                    ('veil-of-the-sanctuary', $$It was woven of blue, purple and scarlet and fine twined linen with cherubim worked on it, and hung by hooks of gold on four gold-overlaid pillars of acacia wood (EXO 26:31-32; 36:35-36). The ark of the testimony stood behind it, so that it divided the holy place from the most holy, while the table, the lampstand and the altar of incense stood outside it (EXO 26:33-35; 40:21, 40:26). Aaron was not to come within it at all times, but on the day of atonement with incense and blood (LEV 16:2, 16:12-15), and when the camp moved it was taken down to cover the ark (NUM 4:5). Solomon made a veil of the same colours for the temple (2CH 3:14), and the veil of the temple was rent from top to bottom when Jesus died (MAT 27:51); whether those belong to this record is left for review. Hebrews calls the most holy place the tabernacle after the second veil and speaks of a way consecrated through the veil, that is, his flesh (HEB 9:3; 10:20).$$,
                     $$It was woven of blue, purple and scarlet and fine twined linen with cherubim worked on it, and hung by hooks of gold on four gold-overlaid pillars of acacia wood (EXO 26:31-32; 36:35-36). The ark of the testimony stood behind it, so that it divided the holy place from the most holy, while the table, the lampstand and the altar of incense stood outside it (EXO 26:33-35; 40:21, 40:26). Aaron was not to come within it at all times, but on the day of atonement with incense and blood (LEV 16:2, 16:12-15), and when the camp moved it was taken down to cover the ark (NUM 4:5). Solomon made a veil of the same colours for the temple (2CH 3:14), and the veil of the temple was rent from top to bottom when Jesus died (MAT 27:51). Hebrews calls the most holy place the tabernacle after the second veil and speaks of a way consecrated through the veil, that is, his flesh (HEB 9:3; 10:20).$$),
                    ('holy-spirit', $$The phrase stands three times in the Hebrew Bible: take not thy holy spirit from me (PSA 51:11), and Israel rebelled and vexed his holy Spirit, and where is he that put his holy Spirit within him (ISA 63:10-11). In the New Testament the Holy Ghost came upon Mary (LUK 1:35), and at Jesus' baptism descended in a bodily shape like a dove upon him (LUK 3:22; MAT 3:16; JHN 1:32-33); Jesus, full of the Holy Ghost, was led by the Spirit into the wilderness (LUK 4:1). Jesus told the disciples to baptize in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Ghost (MAT 28:19), and said that the Holy Ghost, whom the Father will send in my name, shall teach you all things (JHN 14:26). At Pentecost they were all filled with the Holy Ghost and began to speak with other tongues (ACT 2:4). The text says the Holy Ghost spoke by the mouth of David and by Esaias the prophet (ACT 1:16; 28:25), said Separate me Barnabas and Saul (ACT 13:2), and forbade Paul to preach the word in Asia (ACT 16:6); Paul writes that the Spirit itself beareth witness with our spirit and maketh intercession for us (ROM 8:16, 26), and that the body is the temple of the Holy Ghost (1CO 6:19). The words linked here are those the text calls holy spirit or the Spirit of God, and the Spirit the same verse or passage speaks of. The Hebrew Bible's spirit of God and spirit of the LORD are not linked, because the same words name an evil spirit from God (1SA 16:15-16, 23; 18:10) and can be read a wind from God (GEN 1:2); nor are the seven Spirits of God of Revelation (REV 3:1; 4:5; 5:6).$$,
                     $$The phrase stands three times in the Hebrew Bible: take not thy holy spirit from me (PSA 51:11), and Israel rebelled and vexed his holy Spirit, and where is he that put his holy Spirit within him (ISA 63:10-11). In the New Testament the Holy Ghost came upon Mary (LUK 1:35), and at Jesus' baptism descended in a bodily shape like a dove upon him (LUK 3:22; MAT 3:16; JHN 1:32-33); Jesus, full of the Holy Ghost, was led by the Spirit into the wilderness (LUK 4:1). Jesus told the disciples to baptize in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Ghost (MAT 28:19), and said that the Holy Ghost, whom the Father will send in my name, shall teach you all things (JHN 14:26). At Pentecost they were all filled with the Holy Ghost and began to speak with other tongues (ACT 2:4). The text says the Holy Ghost spoke by the mouth of David and by Esaias the prophet (ACT 1:16; 28:25), said Separate me Barnabas and Saul (ACT 13:2), and forbade Paul to preach the word in Asia (ACT 16:6); Paul writes that the Spirit itself beareth witness with our spirit and maketh intercession for us (ROM 8:16, 26), and that the body is the temple of the Holy Ghost (1CO 6:19). In the Hebrew Bible the words for the spirit of God and the spirit of the LORD also name an evil spirit from God (1SA 16:15-16, 23; 18:10) and can be read a wind from God (GEN 1:2). Revelation speaks of the seven Spirits of God (REV 3:1; 4:5; 5:6).$$),
                    ('nathanael', $$Philip found Nathanael and told him, We have found him, of whom Moses in the law, and the prophets, did write, Jesus of Nazareth; and Nathanael said, Can there any good thing come out of Nazareth? (JHN 1:45-46). Jesus said of him, Behold an Israelite indeed, in whom is no guile, and that he had seen him under the fig tree before Philip called him; Nathanael answered, Rabbi, thou art the Son of God; thou art the King of Israel (JHN 1:47-49). After the resurrection he was among the disciples at the sea of Tiberias, where John names him Nathanael of Cana in Galilee (JHN 21:1-2). The name stands only in John. A tradition identifies him with Bartholomew, whom Matthew, Mark and Luke name beside Philip in the lists of the twelve (MAT 10:3; MRK 3:18; LUK 6:14; ACT 1:13); no verse says they are one man, and BibleData, which follows the tradition, holds the name Nathanael on its record of Bartholomew.$$,
                     $$Philip found Nathanael and told him, We have found him, of whom Moses in the law, and the prophets, did write, Jesus of Nazareth; and Nathanael said, Can there any good thing come out of Nazareth? (JHN 1:45-46). Jesus said of him, Behold an Israelite indeed, in whom is no guile, and that he had seen him under the fig tree before Philip called him; Nathanael answered, Rabbi, thou art the Son of God; thou art the King of Israel (JHN 1:47-49). After the resurrection he was among the disciples at the sea of Tiberias, where John names him Nathanael of Cana in Galilee (JHN 21:1-2). The name stands only in John. A tradition identifies him with Bartholomew, whom Matthew, Mark and Luke name beside Philip in the lists of the twelve (MAT 10:3; MRK 3:18; LUK 6:14; ACT 1:13); no verse says they are one man.$$),
                    ('altar-the-lord-is-my-banner', $$Amalek fought with Israel at Rephidim, and Israel prevailed while Moses held up his hands on the hill (EXO 17:8-13). The LORD told Moses to write it for a memorial in a book (EXO 17:14). Moses built an altar and called its name Jehovah-nissi, 'the LORD is my banner' (EXO 17:15), with the saying that the LORD will have war with Amalek from generation to generation (EXO 17:16). The Hebrew has the name as two ordinary words, the LORD and banner; only the word 'altar' is linked here.$$,
                     $$Amalek fought with Israel at Rephidim, and Israel prevailed while Moses held up his hands on the hill (EXO 17:8-13). The LORD told Moses to write it for a memorial in a book (EXO 17:14). Moses built an altar and called its name Jehovah-nissi, 'the LORD is my banner' (EXO 17:15), with the saying that the LORD will have war with Amalek from generation to generation (EXO 17:16). The Hebrew has the name as two ordinary words, the LORD and banner.$$),
                    ('tree-of-life', $$The LORD God made every tree to grow out of the ground, the tree of life also in the midst of the garden, and the tree of knowledge of good and evil (GEN 2:9). When the man had eaten of the tree of knowledge, the LORD God said, lest he put forth his hand, and take also of the tree of life, and eat, and live for ever, and sent him forth from the garden; and he placed at the east of the garden cherubims, and a flaming sword which turned every way, to keep the way of the tree of life (GEN 3:22-24). In Revelation, to him that overcometh is given to eat of the tree of life, which is in the midst of the paradise of God (REV 2:7); on either side of the river of water of life was the tree of life, which bare twelve manner of fruits and yielded her fruit every month, and the leaves of the tree were for the healing of the nations (REV 22:2), and blessed are they that have right to the tree of life (REV 22:14). At REV 22:19 Nestle's edition reads the tree of life where the Textus Receptus and the King James read the book of life. Proverbs calls wisdom, the fruit of the righteous, a desire fulfilled and a wholesome tongue a tree of life (PRO 3:18; 11:30; 13:12; 15:4); those are figures of speech and are not linked.$$,
                     $$The LORD God made every tree to grow out of the ground, the tree of life also in the midst of the garden, and the tree of knowledge of good and evil (GEN 2:9). When the man had eaten of the tree of knowledge, the LORD God said, lest he put forth his hand, and take also of the tree of life, and eat, and live for ever, and sent him forth from the garden; and he placed at the east of the garden cherubims, and a flaming sword which turned every way, to keep the way of the tree of life (GEN 3:22-24). In Revelation, to him that overcometh is given to eat of the tree of life, which is in the midst of the paradise of God (REV 2:7); on either side of the river of water of life was the tree of life, which bare twelve manner of fruits and yielded her fruit every month, and the leaves of the tree were for the healing of the nations (REV 22:2), and blessed are they that have right to the tree of life (REV 22:14). At REV 22:19 Nestle's edition reads the tree of life where the Textus Receptus and the King James read the book of life. Proverbs calls wisdom, the fruit of the righteous, a desire fulfilled and a wholesome tongue a tree of life (PRO 3:18; 11:30; 13:12; 15:4); those are figures of speech.$$),
                    ('benjaminites', $$H3228, which Strong derives from Jamin, is a different people and keeps its own record as the Jaminites; his entry points at H1145 for this one.$$,
                     $$H3228, which Strong derives from Jamin, is a different people, the Jaminites; his entry points at H1145 for this one.$$),
                    ('azariah', $$1 Kings 4:2 names Azariah the son of Zadok the priest first among Solomon's princes; whether he is this priest or Azariah the son of Ahimaaz, Zadok's grandson (1CH 6:9), the text does not say. The Azariah who withstood King Uzziah in the temple (2CH 26:17-20) is held as azariah-20.$$,
                     $$1 Kings 4:2 names Azariah the son of Zadok the priest first among Solomon's princes; whether he is this priest or Azariah the son of Ahimaaz, Zadok's grandson (1CH 6:9), the text does not say. The Azariah who withstood King Uzziah in the temple (2CH 26:17-20) is another man.$$),
                    ('malchijah-2', $$The priest who sealed the covenant (NEH 10:3) and the Malchijah at the dedication of the wall (NEH 12:42) are held apart; the text does not say whether either of them is this man.$$,
                     $$The priest who sealed the covenant (NEH 10:3) and the Malchijah at the dedication of the wall (NEH 12:42) may be other men; the text does not say whether either of them is this man.$$),
                    ('meshullam-13', $$The priest who sealed the covenant (NEH 10:7), the chief of the people who sealed it (NEH 10:20) and the head of the house of Ginnethon (NEH 12:16) are held apart; the text does not say whether any of them is this man.$$,
                     $$The priest who sealed the covenant (NEH 10:7), the chief of the people who sealed it (NEH 10:20) and the head of the house of Ginnethon (NEH 12:16) may be other men; the text does not say whether any of them is this man.$$),
                    ('ahuzzath', $$The Hebrew names Ahuzzath once, as one of Abimelech's friends, when he and Phicol come to Isaac (GEN 26:26). The Septuagint names him in the account of Abraham as well: Ochozath his friend, ὁ νυμφαγωγὸς αὐτοῦ, stands beside Abimelech and Phicol at GEN 21:22 and 21:32, where the Masoretic text has no Ahuzzath. Named in both generations as the king's companion, he may, like Abimelech and Phicol, be a title, that of the king's friend or counsellor, rather than one man's name. That is a supposition the text does not state, and the entry is held as possibly a title so as not to set up one man the text cannot show.$$,
                     $$The Hebrew names Ahuzzath once, as one of Abimelech's friends, when he and Phicol come to Isaac (GEN 26:26). The Septuagint names him in the account of Abraham as well: Ochozath his friend, ὁ νυμφαγωγὸς αὐτοῦ, stands beside Abimelech and Phicol at GEN 21:22 and 21:32, where the Masoretic text has no Ahuzzath. Named in both generations as the king's companion, he may, like Abimelech and Phicol, be a title, that of the king's friend or counsellor, rather than one man's name. That is a supposition the text does not state.$$),
                    ('phicol', $$Phicol comes with Abimelech to Abraham at Beersheba (GEN 21:22, 32) and again, after Abraham's death, with Abimelech to Isaac (GEN 26:26), both times as the commander of his army. One commander serving across both accounts is as hard to hold as one king, and the same pair of names recurring suggests that Phicol, like Abimelech, may be a title, that of the commander of the army of Gerar, rather than one man's name. That is a supposition the text does not state, and the entry is held as possibly a title so as not to set up one man the text cannot show.$$,
                     $$Phicol comes with Abimelech to Abraham at Beersheba (GEN 21:22, 32) and again, after Abraham's death, with Abimelech to Isaac (GEN 26:26), both times as the commander of his army. One commander serving across both accounts is as hard to hold as one king, and the same pair of names recurring suggests that Phicol, like Abimelech, may be a title, that of the commander of the army of Gerar, rather than one man's name. That is a supposition the text does not state.$$),
                    ('governor', $$Two words, one office: the pechah of the Persian provinces, which Haggai gives to Zerubbabel and Ezra to Tatnai and Sheshbazzar, and the hegemon of the Gospels and Acts, the Roman governor of Judaea or Syria. The King James also writes governor for other words, for Joseph's shallit over Egypt (GEN 42:6) and for Gedaliah, whom Nebuchadnezzar set over the cities of Judah (JER 40:5); those are different words and are not joined here.$$,
                     $$Two words, one office: the pechah of the Persian provinces, which Haggai gives to Zerubbabel and Ezra to Tatnai and Sheshbazzar, and the hegemon of the Gospels and Acts, the Roman governor of Judaea or Syria. The King James also writes governor for other words, for Joseph's shallit over Egypt (GEN 42:6) and for Gedaliah, whom Nebuchadnezzar set over the cities of Judah (JER 40:5); those are different words.$$),
                    ('high-priest', $$The Law speaks of the high priest as an office, 'the priest that is great among his brethren' (LEV 21:10), and names him as such only rarely before the exile: Hilkiah under Josiah (2KI 22:4), and after it Joshua son of Josedech (HAG 1:1) and Eliashib (NEH 3:1). In the New Testament the Greek archiereus is both the high priest and, in the plural, the chief priests as a body; the men it names singly are Caiaphas (MAT 26:3), Annas (ACT 4:6), Ananias (ACT 23:2) and, in the Lord's own words, Abiathar (MRK 2:26). Aaron, Eleazar, Eli, Zadok and Jehoiada held the office by every account, but the text calls them the priest rather than the high priest by name, and they are not joined here.$$,
                     $$The Law speaks of the high priest as an office, 'the priest that is great among his brethren' (LEV 21:10), and names him as such only rarely before the exile: Hilkiah under Josiah (2KI 22:4), and after it Joshua son of Josedech (HAG 1:1) and Eliashib (NEH 3:1). In the New Testament the Greek archiereus is both the high priest and, in the plural, the chief priests as a body; the men it names singly are Caiaphas (MAT 26:3), Annas (ACT 4:6), Ananias (ACT 23:2) and, in the Lord's own words, Abiathar (MRK 2:26). Aaron, Eleazar, Eli, Zadok and Jehoiada held the office by every account, but the text calls them the priest rather than the high priest by name.$$),
                    ('rabsaris', $$Akkadian rab sha reshi, the chief of the court officials. The text gives it to an unnamed envoy of Sennacherib (2KI 18:17), and at the fall of Jerusalem to two Babylonian officers by name, Sarsechim (JER 39:3) and Nebushasban (JER 39:13), who hold no record of their own here.$$,
                     $$Akkadian rab sha reshi, the chief of the court officials. The text gives it to an unnamed envoy of Sennacherib (2KI 18:17), and at the fall of Jerusalem to two Babylonian officers by name, Sarsechim (JER 39:3) and Nebushasban (JER 39:13).$$))
                UPDATE entity e
                SET notes = said.now
                FROM said
                WHERE e.slug = said.slug
                  AND e.notes = said.was;
                """);

